# Input, positions and results

This file is the ground the rest of the library stands on: the text being parsed, how far into it
a parser has got, what is skipped as white space, and what a parse answers. It is a literate Avin
file, so everything indented four columns is the program and everything at the margin is this
explanation, which the compiler never reads.

    module dev.avinlang.parsing

## Positions

A parser works in byte offsets, because that is what indexing a string costs nothing for. A person
reading an error wants a line and a column, and a column counted in characters: `é` is two bytes
but one column. A `Position` carries all three, so code that wants to slice the text and code that
wants to show the place to a person each have what they need.

    // A place in a text: the byte offset, and the line and column it falls on, both counted from 1. A
    // column counts characters, not bytes.
    data Position(offset: int, line: int, column: int)

A position shows as `line:column`, the form editors and compilers use, so an error message can be
clicked through to its place.

    impl Display for Position
        display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = out.write(s"$line:$column")

## What a parse answers

A parse ends in one of three ways, and a `ParseResult` says which:

- `Success(result, next)`: the parser read something, and `result` is its value. `next` is the
  input where it stopped.
- `Failure(msg, next)`: no alternative of the grammar could read the text. `next` is the input at
  the furthest place any alternative reached, and `msg` says what could have continued the text
  there and what was found instead.
- `Error(msg, next)`: the same, but final: the grammar passed a commit point (`~!`, `commit`,
  `err`) and then failed, so no other alternative was tried. Its message names what the committed
  rule wanted.

```avin
parseAll(wholeNumber, "12") match
    Success(n, _) -> println(n)
    Failure(msg, next) -> println(s"${next.pos()}: $msg")
    Error(msg, next) -> println(s"${next.pos()}: $msg")
```

The second field of each case is matched by position. `r.next()` reaches it on any result without
taking the result apart.

    // The outcome of a parse: `Success` with the value read, or `Failure` or `Error`, which is a
    // failure past a commit point, with a message. Each holds the input where the parse ended:
    // after the value, or at the furthest place reached.
    data ParseResult[+T]
        Success(result: T, input: Input)
        Failure(msg: string, input: Input)
        Error(msg: string, input: Input)

A result is mostly taken apart by `match`, but the common questions have methods: whether it
succeeded, its value with a default, and its value changed by a function.

    impl[T] ParseResult[T]
        // The input where the parse ended: after the value on success, at the furthest place reached
        // otherwise.
        next() -> Input = self match
            Success(_, n) -> n
            Failure(_, n) -> n
            Error(_, n) -> n

        // Whether the parse succeeded.
        successful() -> bool = self is Success(_, _)

        // Whether the parse failed: the opposite of `successful`.
        isEmpty() -> bool = !successful()

        // The value, which traps when the parse failed.
        get() -> T = self match
            Success(a, _) -> a
            _ -> fail("no result: the parse failed")

        // The value, or `default`, evaluated only when the parse failed.
        getOrElse(default: -> T) -> T = self match
            Success(a, _) -> a
            _ -> default

        // The value given to `f`; a failure stays as it is.
        map[U](f: T -> U) -> ParseResult[U] = self match
            Success(a, n) -> Success(f(a), n)
            Failure(m, n) -> Failure(m, n)
            Error(m, n) -> Error(m, n)

A grammar that recovers from its errors, with `recover` in `parser.lavin`, can succeed and still have
found mistakes in the text. `errors()` lists every one, in the order the parse met them: those it
recovered from, then the failure it ended in, if it did. A parse with nothing to report answers an
empty list, so `r.errors().isEmpty()` is the question "was the text right?", whether or not the
grammar recovers.

        // Every error the parse met, in order: those `recover` recovered from, then the failure the parse
        // ended in, if it ended in one. Empty when the text was read without a mistake.
        errors() -> List[ParseError]
            val ending = self match
                Success(_, _) -> Nil
                Failure(m, n) -> List(ParseError(m, n.pos(), false))
                Error(m, n) -> List(ParseError(m, n.pos(), true))
            next().recovered() ++ ending

An error is its message, its position, and whether it was past a commit point: the `Failure` or
`Error` it would have been had nothing recovered from it. It shows as `line:column: message`, the
form a compiler prints.

    // A mistake in the text: what was expected and found, where, and whether it was past a commit point.
    data ParseError(msg: string, pos: Position, committed: bool)

    impl Display for ParseError
        display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = out.write(s"$pos: $msg")

A result shows as its position, what kind it is, and the value or the message:
`[1:3] parsed: 42`, ``[2:5] failure: expected `,` or `]`, found '4'``.

    impl[T: Display] Display for ParseResult[T]
        display(out: Writer, spec: FormatSpec) = self match
            Success(a, n) -> out.write(s"[${n.pos()}] parsed: $a")
            Failure(m, n) -> out.write(s"[${n.pos()}] failure: $m")
            Error(m, n) -> out.write(s"[${n.pos()}] error: $m")

## The input

An `Input` is the one mutable thing in a parse. Every parser reads from it and moves its offset;
none copies it. That choice is what makes the library cheap: a parser that succeeds allocates only
its value and the `Some` around it, and backtracking is restoring one integer. The alternative
design, an immutable input with every result carrying the input after it, allocates a result at
every step of every parse.

The input also remembers the furthest failure, and whether the parse has passed a **commit point**.
A commit point is where the grammar knows which rule it is in, so that a failure after it is final:
there is no point trying the next alternative from the start, and the error should name what the
committed rule wanted.

Finally it carries the grammar's **white space**: the parser that `literal`, the token parsers,
`positioned` and `phrase` skip before they read, or `None` where nothing is skipped. It comes from
the grammar that started the parse, as `RegexParsers` in `parser.lavin` describes.

    // The text a parser reads and the state of reading it: the byte offset of the next character, the
    // furthest offset at which anything failed with what was expected there, whether a failure has
    // passed a commit point, and the white space skipped before each token. Parsers read and move
    // `offset`; an alternative that fails is retried from where it started.
    class Input(val source: string, val whiteSpace: Option[Parser[unit]] = Some(spaces))
        // The text's length in bytes.
        val length = source.byteLength()
        // The byte offset of the next character to read.
        var offset = 0
        // Set when a parser fails after a commit point: no alternative is tried after it and the whole
        // parse fails.
        var committed = false
        // How many `not` look aheads are running: while any is, nothing expected is recorded, since
        // what a look ahead refuses to see is no part of what the text lacks.
        var lookingAhead = 0

What has failed furthest, and what was expected there, are private: parsers record a failure with
`expect` and `fail` below and never read the record back. `known` is the last position the input
worked out, explained with `position`. `skipping` is set while the white space parser runs, and
`spaceAt` and `spaceWanted` are the last thing it expected, which is what a white space error
reports. `tokenStart`, `tokenEnd` and `tokenWanted` are the last `literal` read, which a `not` just
after it names when it refuses what follows. `found` holds the errors `recover` has recovered from.

        private var furthest = -1
        private val expectations = Buffer[string]()
        // The last position worked out, from which the next one is counted when it lies further on.
        private var known = Position(0, 1, 1)
        // Whether the white space parser is running.
        private var skipping = false
        // Where the white space parser last expected something, and what.
        private var spaceAt = -1
        private var spaceWanted = ""
        // Where the last literal began and ended, after its white space, and what it expected.
        private var tokenStart = -1
        private var tokenEnd = -1
        private var tokenWanted = ""
        // The errors recovered from, in the order they were met.
        private val found = Buffer[ParseError]()

These three are what a hand-written parser needs to look at the text.

        // Whether every character has been read.
        atEnd() -> bool = offset >= length

        // The character at `offset`, which traps at the end of the text.
        first() -> char = source.charAt(offset)

        // The position of `offset`.
        pos() -> Position = position(offset)

### White space

`handleWhiteSpace` moves past the grammar's white space. The white space parser is a parser like
any other, so it may be written with `literal` or `rep`, and those must not skip white space
themselves, or skipping would never end: while it runs, `handleWhiteSpace` does nothing. Nothing it
expects is recorded either. White space ends where it fails to read more, and that failure is no
part of what the text got wrong. A white space parser that fails reads nothing.

White space can be wrong, though: a comment opened and never closed. A white space parser says so
the way any rule does, with a commit point, usually `err(msg)` where the mistake stands:

```avin
lazy val unclosed: Parser[unit] = guard(acceptSeq("/*".chars())) ~> err("`*/` to close the comment that begins here")
```

A committed failure inside white space is an error of the text. Since none of white space's own
failures is part of a message, it is reported as the last thing white space expected before it
committed, where it expected it, so `err(msg)` reports `msg` exactly where it stands. The input is
left committed and back where the white space began, and `handleWhiteSpace` answers `false`, so the
token that was about to be read fails and the parse fails with the error. Every parser that skips
white space checks the answer: a custom one does it as `if !input.handleWhiteSpace() then return
None`.

        // Moves past the grammar's white space, if it skips any. Answers `false`, having recorded the
        // error and committed, where the white space parser fails past a commit point.
        handleWhiteSpace() -> bool
            if skipping then return true
            whiteSpace match
                Some(ws) ->
                    val start = offset
                    val wasCommitted = committed
                    committed = false
                    spaceAt = -1
                    skipping = true
                    val r = ws.run(self)
                    skipping = false
                    if r is None
                        if committed
                            if spaceAt >= 0 then recordAt(spaceAt, spaceWanted)
                            offset = start
                            return false
                        offset = start
                    committed = wasCommitted
                    true
                None -> true

### Recording what was expected

Every leaf parser that fails says what it wanted. Only the furthest offset's wishes are worth
keeping, since the text is right up to there: a failure further on replaces them, a failure at the
same place joins them, and a failure before it is forgotten. Nothing is recorded inside a `not`
either: `if` followed by anything but a letter is the keyword, and the letter `not` looked for is
not something the text should have had.

        // Records that `what` was expected at `offset`. Only the furthest offset's expectations are kept,
        // since the text is right up to there. Nothing is recorded while white space is skipped or a
        // `not` looks ahead.
        expect(what: string)
            if lookingAhead > 0 then return
            if skipping
                spaceAt = offset
                spaceWanted = what
                return
            if offset > furthest
                furthest = offset
                expectations.clear()
                expectations.addOne(what)
            elif offset == furthest
                expectations.addOne(what)

A parser fails by answering `None`, and almost always records why at the same moment, so `fail`
does both and reads as the last line of a parser that gives up: `else input.fail("integer")`.

        // Records that `what` was expected at `offset`, and fails.
        fail[T](what: string) -> Option[T]
            expect(what)
            None

        // Whether the white space parser is running.
        skippingWhiteSpace() -> bool = skipping

        // Records that `what` was expected at offset `at`, the input staying where it is.
        private recordAt(at: int, what: string)
            val here = offset
            offset = at
            expect(what)
            offset = here

A look ahead that refuses what follows a literal, `literal("if") <~ not(elem("letter", c ->
c.isLetter()))` at `iffy`, has found that the literal was not there after all: the `if` of `iffy`
is not the keyword. What `not` looked for is no part of the message, so the literal is: `literal`
notes each token it reads with `readToken`, and `not`, failing right where that token ended, calls
`refuseToken`, which records the literal as expected where it began. The message then reads
``expected `if`, found 'i'``, as it would had the text held no `if` at all. A literal read inside a
look ahead is not noted, since the look ahead reads nothing.

        // Notes that a literal expecting `what` was read from `start` to `offset`.
        readToken(start: int, what: string)
            if lookingAhead > 0 then return
            tokenStart = start
            tokenEnd = offset
            tokenWanted = what

        // Records the literal that ended at `at`, if one did, as expected where it began: a look ahead
        // at `at` has refused what follows it.
        refuseToken(at: int)
            if tokenEnd == at && tokenStart >= 0 then recordAt(tokenStart, tokenWanted)

### Failure messages

A rule made of many parts fails with the expectations of its parts: an expression that fails at its
first character expects an integer, `(`, `-`, and whatever else can begin one. Often the better
message names the rule instead: *expected expression*. `withFailureMessage` does that, but only for
a failure that got no further than the rule's first character. A failure further in has
expectations that say more than the rule's name.

Replacing works in two steps. Before the rule runs, the input notes how many expectations are
already recorded at its start. If the rule fails and nothing got further, the ones the rule added
are dropped and the message takes their place.

        // The number of expectations recorded at offset `at`: -1 when nothing has failed that far yet.
        expectationsAt(at: int) -> int = if furthest < at then -1 else expectations.length()

        // Replaces what was recorded at offset `at` since `mark` (an `expectationsAt(at)` taken before)
        // by `what`: a failure that got no further than `at` is reported under one name.
        relabel(at: int, mark: int, what: string)
            if furthest != at || lookingAhead > 0 then return
            val keep = if mark < 0 then 0 else mark
            while expectations.length() > keep
                expectations.remove(expectations.length() - 1)
            expectations.addOne(what)

A committed failure is final wherever it happened, so `withErrorMessage` replaces everything
expected at the furthest place.

        // Replaces everything expected at the furthest place reached by `what`.
        reword(what: string)
            if lookingAhead > 0 then return
            if skipping
                if spaceAt >= 0 then spaceWanted = what
                return
            if furthest < 0 then return
            expectations.clear()
            expectations.addOne(what)

### Lines and columns

A position is worked out only when it is asked for: for an error, once, and for `positioned`, once
per value. Counting from the start of the text each time would make a parse that asks for many
positions quadratic. The input keeps the last position it worked out and counts on from there when
the next one is further along, so positions asked for in order cost one pass over the text in all.

A column counts characters. In UTF-8 every byte of the form `10xxxxxx` continues a character, so a
character starts at every other byte, and only those advance the column.

        // The position of byte offset `at`, counted from the last position worked out when `at` lies
        // after it, so positions asked for in order cost one pass over the text.
        position(at: int) -> Position
            val from = if at >= known.offset then known else Position(0, 1, 1)
            var i = from.offset
            var line = from.line
            var column = from.column
            while i < at
                int(source.byteAt(i)) match
                    0x0a ->
                        line += 1
                        column = 1
                    0x80..0xbf -> ()
                    _ -> column += 1
                i += 1
            known = Position(at, line, column)
            known

### The result

When the parse is over, what the parser answered becomes the result. A failure is reported at the
furthest place reached, and the input is left there as the result's `next`.

The expectations are listed in the order the grammar tried them, which is the order its author wrote
the alternatives in, and once each, since two alternatives can expect the same thing at the same
place, as `a ~ b | a ~ b ~ c` does. They are joined the way a sentence joins them: `a`, `a or b`,
`a, b or c`. What was found is the character's `debug` form, quoted and escaped, so a newline shows
as `'\n'` rather than breaking the message across lines. A failure that expected nothing in
particular, which happens when a look ahead refuses a character, says only what it found.

        // What a parse that answered `r` reports: its value, or the furthest failure with every distinct
        // expectation there, an `Error` when a commit point was passed.
        result[T](r: Option[T]) -> ParseResult[T]
            r match
                Some(a) -> Success(a, self)
                None ->
                    val at = if furthest < 0 then offset else furthest
                    val msg = message(at, true)
                    offset = at
                    if committed then Error(msg, self) else Failure(msg, self)

        // What a failure at `at` says: everything expected there, when `expected` is set, and what was
        // found.
        private message(at: int, expected: bool) -> string
            val seen = if expected then expectations.distinct() else Vector.empty[string]()
            val there = if at >= length then "end of input" else debug(source.charAt(at))
            seen.length() match
                0 -> s"unexpected $there"
                1 -> s"expected ${seen(0)}, found $there"
                _ -> s"expected ${seen.init().mkString(", ")} or ${seen.last()}, found $there"

### Recovering from an error

`recover` in `parser.lavin` turns a failure into an error the parse goes on past. The input does
the bookkeeping, since the record of what was expected is its own. `recoverFrom` takes the failure
of a rule that began at `start` as the message the parse would have ended with, at the furthest place
reached. A rule that failed without recording anything at or past its start, as a look ahead that
refuses does, is reported at its start as finding something unexpected. The error is kept in the
list the result's `errors()` answers, unless the error just before it is at the same place: a
`recover` that resynchronised without moving, and fails there again, has found one mistake, not
two. Then the record is cleared, the expectations and the commit both, so the next failure is
reported on its own and the alternatives above are tried again, and the resynchronisation skips
from where the error was.

Two failures are not taken, and leave the record, the errors and the offset as they were, so that
the rules around the `recover` go on as though it had not been tried. One at the end of the text has
nothing after it to resume reading; the result reports it in the end as the failure the parse ended
in, with everything the grammar expected there. And one whose resynchronisation fails is refused by
the grammar: a statement's skip that will not pass the start of a declaration leaves the error to
the declaration's rule.

        // Takes the failure of a rule that began at `start` as an error recovered from, skipping from
        // where it was with `sync`: keeps the error, clears the record of what was expected and the
        // commit, and answers the error. Answers `None`, the record and the offset as they were, where
        // the failure is at the end of the text or `sync` fails.
        recoverFrom[U](start: int, sync: Parser[U]) -> Option[ParseError]
            val reached = furthest >= start
            val at = if reached then furthest else start
            if at >= length then return None
            val e = ParseError(message(at, reached), position(at), committed)
            val failedAt = offset
            val keptFurthest = furthest
            val keptExpected = expectations.toList()
            val keptCommitted = committed
            val mark = found.length()
            if found.lastOption().forall(prev -> prev.pos.offset != at) then found.addOne(e)
            forget()
            offset = at
            if sync.run(self) is None
                dropErrors(mark)
                furthest = keptFurthest
                expectations.clear()
                expectations.addAll(keptExpected)
                committed = keptCommitted
                offset = failedAt
                return None
            forget()
            Some(e)

        // Clears the record of what was expected, and the commit.
        private forget()
            furthest = -1
            expectations.clear()
            committed = false

An alternative that is abandoned takes the errors it recovered from with it: the grammar tried
`p | q`, `p` recovered from a mistake and then failed, and `q` read the text instead, so the
mistake was in a reading of the text that was not kept. The combinators that backtrack note how
many errors there were before they tried, with `errorCount`, and drop the rest with `dropErrors`
when they go back.

        // The errors recovered from so far, in the order they were met.
        recovered() -> List[ParseError] = found.toList()

        // How many errors have been recovered from.
        errorCount() -> int = found.length()

        // Drops the errors recovered from after the first `count`.
        dropErrors(count: int)
            while found.length() > count
                found.remove(found.length() - 1)

        // Takes out the errors recovered from after the first `count`, answering them.
        takeErrors(count: int) -> List[ParseError]
            val taken = found.drop(count).toList()
            dropErrors(count)
            taken

        // Adds `es` to the errors recovered from.
        addErrors(es: List[ParseError]) = found.addAll(es)
